  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Assistance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In October 2024, tropical cyclone Trami (local name: Kristine) brought heavy intense rains across the Philippines and severely impacted housing, livelihood, and government facilities, including critical health facilities in the Bicol Region. The damage to these public health facilities significantly hindered access to sexual and reproductive health (SRH) services for women and girls, who are among the most vulnerable populations during crises.    
&#x200d;
With the support of Australia&#x2019;s Sexual and Reproductive Health Program in Crisis and Post-Crisis Situations (SPRINT) program, the Family Planning Organization of the Philippines (FPOP) delivered life-saving SRH services to more than 16,000 people affected by the crisis. The response prevented sexual violence and respond to the needs of survivors, decrease maternal and newborn mortality, reduce the transmission of HIV and sexually transmitted infections (STIs) and prevent unwanted pregnancy.  
&#x200d;
Medical teams delivered a total of 2,167 obstetric and gynaecological services, including antenatal care to 842 pregnant women and post-natal care to 1,119 new mothers. In addition, a total of 3,949 services related to gender-based services were provided, alongside other essential services &#x2013; such as emergency contraception, testing and treatment for HIV and STIs, and referrals to further services &#x2013; to support survivors and their wellbeing.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In September 2024, heavy intense rainfall across Nepal triggered widespread flooding and landslides. Damage to housing and other infrastructure hindered access to sexual and reproductive health (SRH) services for women and girls, who are among the most vulnerable populations during crises. 
&#x200d;
With the support of Australia&#x2019;s SPRINT program, the Family Planning Association of Nepal (FPAN) organised 25 mobile medical centres, delivered emergency SRH services to 3,000 people affected by the crisis. The response aimed to prevent sexual violence and respond to the needs of survivors, decrease maternal and newborn mortality, reduce the transmission of HIV and sexually transmitted infections (STIs) and prevent unwanted pregnancy. 
&#x200d;
By providing access to modern contraceptives to 551 people affected by the crisis, the response provided an estimated 1,426 couple years of protection and averted an estimated 155 unintended pregnancies.
&#x200d;
In addition, the medical teams also delivered antenatal care to 403 pregnant women and post-natal care to 675 new mothers. A total of 741 services related to gender-based violence were provided, including clinical management of rape, emergency contraceptives, psychosocial counselling and referrals. The medical teams also provided testing and treatment for HIV and other STIs, and distributed more than 17,000 condoms.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
